    "text": "We are given:\n\n$$\np = \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^2}, \\quad q = \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3}\n$$\n\nand we are to express the double sum:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3}\n$$\n\nin terms of $ p $ and $ q $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Change of Variables\n\nLet’s define a new variable $ n = j + k $. Then for each fixed $ n \\geq 2 $, the number of pairs $ (j, k) $ such that $ j + k = n $ is $ n - 1 $, because $ j $ can range from 1 to $ n - 1 $, and $ k = n - j $.\n\nSo we can rewrite the double sum as:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} = \\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^{n - 1} \\frac{1}{n^3}\n$$\n\nNow, for each fixed $ n $, the inner sum is:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j = 1}^{n - 1} \\frac{1}{n^3} = \\frac{n - 1}{n^3}\n$$\n\nSo the entire sum becomes:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{n - 1}{n^3}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Simplify the Sum\n\nWe now compute:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{n - 1}{n^3} = \\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{n}{n^3} - \\frac{1}{n^3} \\right) = \\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{n^2} - \\frac{1}{n^3} \\right)\n$$\n\nNow split the sum:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} - \\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3}\n$$\n\nRecall that:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = p, \\quad \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3} = q\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = p - \\frac{1}{1^2} = p - 1\n$$\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3} = q - \\frac{1}{1^3} = q - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original double sum becomes:\n\n$$\n(p - 1) - (q - 1) = p - q\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{p - q}\n$$",
