    "text": "To determine which student has the **greatest average speed**, we need to understand the relationship between **distance** and **time** from the graph.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the Graph\n\n- The **x-axis** represents **time**.\n- The **y-axis** represents **distance**.\n- Each point on the graph corresponds to a student's position at a certain time.\n- The **average speed** of a student is calculated as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Average Speed} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Extract the Data from the Graph\n\nFrom the Asymptote code, we can extract the coordinates of the points:\n\n- **Evelyn**: (1.25, 4.5)\n- **Briana**: (2.5, 2.2)\n- **Carla**: (4.25, 5.2)\n- **Debra**: (5.6, 2.8)\n- **Angela**: (6.8, 1.4)\n\nEach point is of the form (time, distance), so we can write:\n\n| Student   | Time (x) | Distance (y) |\n|-----------|----------|--------------|\n| Evelyn    | 1.25     | 4.5          |\n| Briana    | 2.5      | 2.2          |\n| Carla     | 4.25     | 5.2          |\n| Debra     | 5.6      | 2.8          |\n| Angela    | 6.8      | 1.4          |\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate Average Speed for Each Student\n\n$$\n\\text{Average Speed} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}}\n$$\n\n- **Evelyn**: $ \\frac{4.5}{1.25} = 3.6 $\n- **Briana**: $ \\frac{2.2}{2.5} = 0.88 $\n- **Carla**: $ \\frac{5.2}{4.25} \\approx 1.2235 $\n- **Debra**: $ \\frac{2.8}{5.6} = 0.5 $\n- **Angela**: $ \\frac{1.4}{6.8} \\approx 0.2059 $\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Compare the Speeds\n\n| Student   | Average Speed |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| Evelyn    | 3.6           |\n| Briana    | 0.88          |\n| Carla     | 1.2235        |\n| Debra     | 0.5           |\n| Angela    | 0.2059        |\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Identify the Student with the Greatest Average Speed\n\nThe student with the **greatest average speed** is **Evelyn**, with an average speed of **3.6**.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Evelyn}}\n$$",
